/* ==================================================================
    static, private, protected
   ================================================================== */


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    16. Static fields?

    a) (R) Only `static func` in 0.3. Constants belong to namespaces
           (0.5), and shared mutable state is better avoided.
    b)     Also `static con` and `static mut` fields.
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

class Temperature
{
    con celsius = 0.0

    func init(celsius: Float)
    {
        this.celsius = celsius
    }

    static func from_fahrenheit(f: Float) -> Temperature
    {
        return Temperature((f - 32.0) * 5.0 / 9.0)
    }
}

// CHOICE:


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    17. Are static methods inherited?

    a) (R) No: `Dog.create()` only exists if Dog declares it. A static
           method has no `this`, so inheriting it mostly causes surprises
           (it would still create an Animal).
    b)     Yes, callable as `Dog.create()`.
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

// CHOICE:


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    18. `private` before namespaces

    Namespaces come in 0.5, and a program is one file until then.

    a) (R) In 0.3, `private` and `protected` only go on fields and
           methods. `private func` at the top level is an error until
           namespaces exist ("private is for class members in 0.3").
    b)     Allow it on top-level functions too, meaning "this file", which
           does nothing until 0.5.
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

class Account
{
    mut balance = 0
    private mut pin = ""

    protected func fee() -> Int
    {
        return 1
    }
}

// CHOICE:


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    19. Can a child see its parent's `private` members?

    a) (R) No. `private` is only the class itself; `protected` is for
           children. Otherwise the two would be the same.
    b)     Yes.
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

// CHOICE:


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    20. Can another object of the same class use private members?

    a) (R) Yes: inside Account's methods, `other.pin` works for any
           Account `other` (like Java, C#, Go). Needed for `equals`-style
           methods.
    b)     No, only through `this`.
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

// CHOICE:


/* ==================================================================
    Enums with values
   ================================================================== */


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    21. Which value types?

    a) (R) `Int` and `String`, the useful ones for databases and JSON.
    b)     Any type that can be a literal: Int, Float, Bool, String.
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

enum Role: String
{
    Admin = "admin"
    Member = "member"
}

enum Priority: Int
{
    Low = 1
    Normal = 5
    High = 10
}

// CHOICE:


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    22. Must every case have a value?

    a) (R) Yes, written out. No automatic numbering: values usually
           end up stored somewhere, so they should be visible and not
           change when a case is added in the middle. Two cases with the
           same value is an error.
    b)     For `Int`, missing values count up from the one before (or 0).
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

// CHOICE:


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    23. Getting the value, and back

    a) (R) `r.value` gives the value (a String for Role). The other way,
           `Role.from("admin")` gives a `Role?`: null if no case has that
           value. `print(r)` still shows the case name, `Admin`.
    b)     `String(r)` gives the value instead of the name, and
           `Role("admin")` converts back.
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

func parse_role(s: String) -> Role
{
    return Role.from(s) else Role.Member
}

// CHOICE:


/* ==================================================================
    Parameter modes
   ================================================================== */


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    24. `mut T` and `copy T` in 0.3?

    doc/explorations/references.brz tries out: a plain parameter is read
    only, `a: mut T` may change the caller's object, `a: copy T` gets
    its own copy, and `mut func` marks methods that change `this`.

    This changes the meaning of 0.2 programs: today a plain List
    parameter can be appended to. Under a), `insertion_sort(xs:
    List<Int>)` in examples/algorithms.brz must become `xs: mut
    List<Int>`, and every method that sets a field must become a
    `mut func`.

    a)     As designed, now: read-only by default, with `mut T`, `copy T`
           and `mut func`. The checker enforces it. 0.2 programs that
           change a parameter need `mut` added (the error says where).
    b) (R) Only `copy T` in 0.3, and decide read-only-by-default
           separately, after trying it on domain models. `copy` is useful
           on its own and breaks nothing. Read-only by default touches
           every signature, so it should be a decision of its own.
    c)     Neither in 0.3; move parameter modes to 0.4.
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

func deposit(a: mut Account, amount: Int)
{
    a.balance += amount
}

func preview(a: copy Account) -> Int
{
    a.balance += 100                       // only the copy changes
    return a.balance
}

// CHOICE:


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    25. If `copy T` comes in 0.3: shallow or deep?

    a) (R) Deep for lists, maps and data classes (all the way down), and
           an error for plain classes: "a class can't be copied; use a
           data class". A plain class may hold things like connections,
           and copying those silently would be wrong.
    b)     Shallow: a new object, but the fields still point to the same
           lists and objects.
    c)     Deep for everything.
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

// CHOICE:


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
    26. If read-only by default (24 a): mark `mut` at the call too?

    a)     Yes: `deposit(mut acc, 100)`. Changes are visible where they
           happen.
    b) (R) No: the signature says it, and the LSP can show it.
------------------------------------------------------------------ */

// CHOICE:


